00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.130
So without further
ado, let's get

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.060
to answering your questions.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.150
The first question
is about nature,

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.640
in particular about the
scientifically-supported

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.580
benefits of getting
outdoors into nature.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.370
The person asks about
the role of sunlight,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.530
the role of calming
sounds, the role

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.070
of observing wildlife, of
observing green colors,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.020
and, quote, "other stuff."

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.570
And in fact, I'm glad that
they asked about "other stuff"

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.660
because I get the question about
the scientifically-supported

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.760
benefits of nature a lot.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.365
I often also get the
question about grounding.

00:02:14.365 --> 00:02:16.740
For those of you that aren't
familiar with what grounding

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.100
is, grounding is a practice of
putting your feet on the Earth

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.220
directly with bare
feet, oftentimes

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.080
into soil or on a lawn.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.080
And it's a question that I
seem to get more and more.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.620
In fact, every week for quite a
long while now, on social media

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.560
or elsewhere, somebody asks me
about the scientific support

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.420
for this practice of grounding.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.580
So obviously there's
a lot of interest

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.540
in what the scientific research
says about getting into nature

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.550
and putting one's
feet on the ground,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.220
a.k.a. grounding, and so on.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.710
OK, so if I'm going to
answer this question,

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.590
I first have to be
very direct with you.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:56.130
There is excellent,
meaning dozens, if not

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.460
hundreds, of quality
peer-reviewed studies which

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.580
support the value of getting
sunlight in one's eyes,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.243
in particular early in the day
to set your circadian rhythm.

00:03:05.243 --> 00:03:07.410
This is something that I've
talked about extensively

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.380
on the Huberman Lab podcast and
as a guest on other podcasts.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.720
It's one of the first and,
frankly, most important

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.350
items on the toolkit for sleep,
which is a zero-cost toolkit

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.630
that you can access by
going to hubermanlab.com,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.340
going to the menu,
going to Newsletter.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.630
You can see it as a PDF there.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.040
You don't have to sign
up for the newsletter.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:24.832
You can just access
that toolkit for sleep.

00:03:24.832 --> 00:03:28.050
And you'll notice that very
close to the top of that list,

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.080
if not top of that list, is
to get sunlight in your eyes

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:31.800
early in the day.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.230
You don't have to see the
sun cross the horizon.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.490
If you can, that's great.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.410
But if you wake up after
the sun has already

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.983
risen, go outside,
face the sun, blink

00:03:39.983 --> 00:03:41.400
as necessary to
protect your eyes,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.470
but get some sunlight
into your eyes

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.380
every single day or as
often as you possibly can,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.830
especially on overcast days.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.170
That's an absolutely
unequivocally

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.400
science-supported tool that will
increase daytime mood, focus,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.198
and alertness and will
improve your nighttime sleep.

00:03:59.198 --> 00:04:01.740
Viewing morning sunlight also
has profoundly positive effects

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.040
on metabolism.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.740
Now, those effects
on metabolism could

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.920
be the direct effect of viewing
sunlight or, more likely,

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.420
they are the
indirect consequence

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.980
of getting better
sleep at night.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.920
So getting sunlight in
your eyes early in the day

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.620
and ideally in the
evening or late afternoon

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.000
before the sun sets is a
very well supported protocol

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.670
that we know is beneficial
for numerous aspects

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.290
of mental health, physical
health, and performance.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.130
And of course, sunlight and
getting sunlight in one's eyes

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.720
does require that
you get outside.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.210
You simply will not
derive the same benefits

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.310
from viewing sunlight
if you try and do it

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.290
through a window or
windshield or if you

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.000
look at a picture of
a sun on a screen.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:39.600
Forget it.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.820
You are not going to set
your circadian rhythm.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.310
You are not going to derive
all the positive effects

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:48.000
of sunlight by trying
to get it from screens

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.143
or from looking at pictures of
suns or something of that sort.

00:04:51.143 --> 00:04:53.310
Now, if you are somebody
that, for whatever reason--

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.050
seasonality, where you live on
the planet, work schedule, et

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:57.640
cetera--

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.780
who cannot get sunlight in
your eyes early in the day

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.540
on a consistent basis, well,
then you might consider

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.420
purchasing a so-called SAD lamp,
which is for seasonal affective

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.670
disorder, which is very
bright light that you can look

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.040
at in the morning for usually
about 5 to 10 minutes.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.853
People will put it
on their counter

00:05:12.853 --> 00:05:15.270
while they make their morning
coffee, eat their breakfast.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.880
And that has also been shown
to improve mood, focus,

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.950
and alertness and set
one's circadian rhythm.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.518
But it is not nearly as
effective as getting sunlight

00:05:23.518 --> 00:05:24.060
in your eyes.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.530
Now, why am I talking
about this practice

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.030
that I've already
talked about extensively

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.380
on numerous podcasts before?

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.172
Well, because the
question is about nature,

00:05:30.172 --> 00:05:33.570
and sunlight is a key feature
of our natural environment.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.790
But the person is also
asking about other features

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.280
of nature-- seeing green
colors, or blue colors,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.150
or running streams
for that matter.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.230
Well, here, too, we can ask,
what does the scientific data

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.380
really say about
things like going

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.933
near a waterfall, or a running
stream, or being near an ocean?

00:05:49.933 --> 00:05:51.600
And actually, this
is quite interesting.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.760
There is actually a
peer-reviewed literature

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.830
on negative ionization,
as it's called,

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:58.800
which is a pattern
of ionization that's

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.700
present close to bodies of water
and particular types of bodies

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.150
of water, such as waterfalls,
running streams, et cetera.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.730
There's actually a laboratory
at Columbia University School

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.430
of Medicine that has
published fairly extensively

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.280
on the health benefits
of negative ionization

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.050
as it relates to
setting circadian

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:19.510
rhythm and some other aspects
of mental and physical health.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.540
I intend to host the
head of that laboratory

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.310
on the Huberman Lab podcast
in the not too distant future.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.130
For now, we can
safely say this--

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.230
there does seem to be
some positive health

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.440
benefits to placing oneself
near bodies of water,

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.960
in particular moving
bodies of water.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.960
And of course, as is always
the case when there's

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.720
a discovery about how the
natural world can impact

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.390
health, there have been
some technologies developed

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.790
to create negative
patterns of ionization

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.300
within a home environment.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.240
But as with viewing
sunlight exposure

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.760
and comparing it
to, say, SAD lamps,

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.270
the negative ionization machines
that one can purchase and put

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.970
in their home environment have
been shown in a few studies

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:58.950
to produce some positive
health benefits.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.740
But those positive
effects in no way

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.450
reach the level of
positive effects

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.670
that have been demonstrated
in studies where people

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.200
are actually
spending a dedicated

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.480
period of time outdoors as
near a moving body of water.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.050
So in thinking about nature,
natural environments,

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.720
there's strong evidence for
getting sunlight in one's eyes.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.140
There is some evidence for being
near moving bodies of water,

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:20.820
perhaps--

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.612
again, I really want
to highlight perhaps--

00:07:22.612 --> 00:07:25.320
because of negative ionization
created by those moving

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:26.580
bodies of water.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.150
There is far less evidence
for sunlight simulators

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.450
or negative ionization
machines used indoors.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.810
And then the asker of this
question also, quite correctly,

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.060
asked about things
like calming sounds,

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.610
watching wildlife,
green colors, et cetera.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.460
And herein lies a really
important point for everyone

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.540
to digest.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.480
While, of course,
answering a question

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.850
about the natural
world or about health

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.910
requires that we first pose a
hypothesis-- for those of you

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.680
that aren't familiar
what a hypothesis is,

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.892
a hypothesis is a
stated prediction.

00:07:58.892 --> 00:07:59.850
So it's not a question.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.650
A question would
be something like,

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.230
is getting out into nature
good for our health?

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:08.730
A scientific hypothesis is where
one actually takes a stance.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.800
For instance, you
could take the stance

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.990
and make the hypothesis
that getting out

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:16.410
into nature for 30 minutes
per day, three days per week,

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.420
improves mood and
nighttime sleep.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.840
So that's a hypothesis
that then one would go on

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.930
to design an experiment
to test and then evaluate

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.850
the data from that
experiment and compare it

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:30.540
to the hypothesis, either
validating or negating

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.800
that hypothesis.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.789
That's essentially
how science is done.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:36.059
There's a lot more to it,
but that's essentially

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:37.440
the scientific method.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.600
And while, of course,
the scientific method

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.130
is a fabulously powerful
tool, for some questions,

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.870
it is a less potent tool.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.050
And the question of is
getting out into nature

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:52.380
helpful for enhancing our
mental and physical health is

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.750
the sort of question
that while ideally you

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:58.140
could design a really well
controlled study to address,

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.680
it's actually quite difficult
to design such a study.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:02.640
And here's why.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.590
In order to perform a study
that's very well controlled,

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:08.010
meaning where you can isolate
individual variables--

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:11.370
like sunlight, like the sorts
of color contrast that one sees

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.430
in a natural scene outdoors
in a forest or near a river--

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:15.960
in order to address
whether or not

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.660
the calming sounds or the
presence of squirrels running

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.510
through your environment
are the relevant factors,

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:24.750
it becomes incredibly
difficult to try and isolate

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.390
individual variables, meaning
as soon as you bring people

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:30.030
into the laboratory, yes, you
have more control over which

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.460
variables, as they're
called, you present them,

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.860
by bringing them into a
room that essentially has

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.777
no art on the walls and
then having them look

00:09:36.777 --> 00:09:38.610
at a picture of a sun
or looking at sunlight

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.740
or listening to soothing
sounds or looking

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.480
at a picture of a
forest, of course,

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.100
you're controlling the
individual variables.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.180
However, there is
a sort of gestalt,

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:51.180
meaning a collective
picture of being

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.890
in nature that
brings together lots

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:55.088
of different elements--
the element of surprise,

00:09:55.088 --> 00:09:55.630
for instance.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.380
The other day I was
out for a Sunday hike,

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.430
and that morning I
saw a squirrel running

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.258
across my path, and
it was interesting

00:10:01.258 --> 00:10:03.300
because the squirrel had
a pinecone in its mouth.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.010
It had been chewed
down to the cob.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:08.140
And the squirrel was probably
only about 7 or 8 inches long,

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.170
and the pine cone was probably
about 9 or 10 inches long--

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:13.030
the cob of the
pine cone, that is.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:15.400
And the interesting thing
is that the squirrel

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:17.837
was carrying it long ways
from the tip of the cob.

00:10:17.837 --> 00:10:20.170
And so I delighted in the
fact that this little squirrel

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:23.650
was working so hard to carry
this object through the woods.

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:26.288
And this object was literally
longer than its own body

00:10:26.288 --> 00:10:28.330
length, and it looked so
dedicated in its running

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:30.760
across the path in
order to do whatever it

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.040
would with that pine cone cob.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.250
So something like that
obviously stuck in my memory.

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:37.030
It delighted me.

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.430
And at the very same
time, there were a number

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.770
of other things happening
besides the presence

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:44.047
of that novel
wildlife experience.

00:10:44.047 --> 00:10:45.380
There was the sound of a stream.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:46.255
There's the sunlight.

00:10:46.255 --> 00:10:48.280
There's the color
contrast everywhere.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:49.870
I'm breathing
fresh air because I

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.780
was far away from any cars
or any civilization, in fact.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:54.970
And so here's what we know.

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:56.800
There are dozens,
if not hundreds,

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.380
of studies that show
that if people get out

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:01.040
of doors into nature--

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:01.900
this could be parks.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.340
This could be near a stream.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.260
This could be an
ocean, any number

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:06.990
of different natural
environments.

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.740
And if they do that for
anywhere from 10 to 30 minutes,

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:13.400
three to seven days
per week, indeed there

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.620
are demonstrated
significant reductions

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.750
in things like blood
pressure, resting heart

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:20.280
rate, improvements in
sleep, improvements in mood.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:24.020
And so I think we can very
reliably say that, yes,

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:28.670
or perhaps even absolutely yes,
getting outside into nature

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:32.450
can enhance various aspects of
mental health, physical health,

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:35.610
and thereby performance in
different aspects of life.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.460
However, when talking
about the benefits

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.330
of getting into nature, we are
talking about hundreds, if not

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:44.790
thousands, of variables, some
of which we are aware of,

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:49.190
such as the presence of wildlife
or sunlight or color contrast.

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:50.690
And then, of course,
there are going

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:52.580
to be dozens, if not
hundreds, maybe even

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:56.090
thousands of other variables
that we're not even aware of.

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:58.130
Perhaps it's
negative ionization.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.560
Most people aren't measuring
the ionization of the air

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:01.910
when they go out into nature.

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:04.160
But perhaps it's
also the presence

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.120
of certain smells from the
soils that are being broken down

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:10.670
and then they're changing the
oxygenation state of the air

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:12.270
around you, the
plants, et cetera.

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:15.830
Again, so many
variables, that, frankly,

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.230
to try and isolate any
one of those variables

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.800
in the laboratory seems
not just artificial,

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:23.540
but I think that it
actually would just

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.880
lead to a diminished sense of
just how valuable nature is.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.240
So while, of course,
the Huberman Lab podcast

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.190
is a podcast where
we always center

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:33.060
on science and
science-related tools,

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:34.850
meaning protocols
that are grounded

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:37.850
in quality peer-reviewed
studies that have been subjected

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:41.630
to control conditions where
some people are getting,

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:44.000
say, the drug treatment
or taking the supplement

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.640
or doing a particular behavioral
practice and other people

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.310
are not or doing some variant of
those and dose response curves,

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:51.740
all of that stuff, when it
comes to the question of

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:54.140
whether or not it's valuable
to get out into nature,

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.930
I think it's a very
straightforward yes.

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:57.950
Absolutely yes.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:01.740
Get out into nature as often
as you can and safely can,

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:02.240
of course.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.190
I realize some of this
is weather permitting.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:05.300
People live in different areas.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:06.110
Some people are in cities.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:06.950
Some people are in deserts.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:08.242
Some people are near the ocean.

00:13:08.242 --> 00:13:11.810
But getting out into nature has
been shown over and over again

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:14.180
to have numerous
positive health effects.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:17.510
And yet, unless we're talking
about sunlight exposure

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.540
and isolating the variable
of setting one's circadian

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:24.080
rhythm by viewing sunlight
early in the day, all

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.230
of the other features of
getting out into nature,

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:29.330
things like forest bathing--

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:32.090
this is a term coined from some,
frankly, pretty nice studies

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:35.360
that were done in Japan in
which people placed themselves

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:38.082
into forest-like environments
for a certain period of time.

00:13:38.082 --> 00:13:40.040
There were control groups
where people were not

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:41.957
placed into those
environments, and the people

00:13:41.957 --> 00:13:44.360
that did this so-called
forest bathing experienced

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:46.790
enhanced mental and
physical health.

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:50.210
That brought on a practice of
people who could not get out

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.310
of doors into forests
bringing plants

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:55.010
into their home environment,
which I think all of us

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:57.240
would agree look nice.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.283
They often will add
pleasant odors to the air,

00:13:59.283 --> 00:14:01.700
and perhaps they do actually
shift our mental and physical

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:02.898
health in significant ways.

00:14:02.898 --> 00:14:04.940
I suppose it depends on
how much you like plants,

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:06.350
how much you pay attention
to them, and, of course,

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:07.880
how many plants there are.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:10.550
But-- and I think this
is a really important

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:12.470
"but" to emphasize--

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:16.610
while most all questions
about tools and protocols

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:20.360
for enhancing health
immediately lead me to say, ah,

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.840
this study or that
study or, yes,

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.340
there's evidence or, no, there
isn't evidence, when it comes

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:28.070
to questions about nature
and grounding in particular,

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:32.120
I take the stance that this
is a unique instance where

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.730
we know there are just so
many benefits of getting out

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.500
into nature that
trying to isolate

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.590
any one of those
variables in a quality,

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:42.350
rigorous way within
the laboratory

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:45.140
almost seems too artificial
to really justify

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:46.310
the conclusions that arrive.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:49.323
Now, I'm sure there are some of
you out there who are aware--

00:14:49.323 --> 00:14:50.990
and if you're not,
I'll tell you-- there

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:53.360
are studies that have
explored this practice

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:54.620
of so-called grounding.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:56.412
They've had people come
into the laboratory

00:14:56.412 --> 00:14:59.273
and place their feet on soil
that is contained within a box

00:14:59.273 --> 00:15:01.190
or there are other studies
where they actually

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:03.190
have people go out of
doors and place their feet

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:04.710
onto the grass or the ground.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.085
And there are a
bunch of theories

00:15:06.085 --> 00:15:07.880
as to how grounding
could improve

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:10.850
one's mental and physical health
that aren't just about getting

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:11.430
outside.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:14.720
So the theories go
that this has to do

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:17.407
with the exchange of
electrons with the Earth

00:15:17.407 --> 00:15:18.990
and the Earth's
surface in particular.

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:21.110
There's been the
argument made that shoes,

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:22.970
in particular shoes
that have rubber soles,

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:25.280
may block some of
this electron exchange

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.987
with the surface of the Earth.

00:15:26.987 --> 00:15:28.820
There have been theories
about the tactile--

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:31.880
that is, the touch sensation--
with the Earth being important.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:35.090
Not a lot of science
published in, let's just say,

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:36.680
blue ribbon journals,
which is not

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:39.305
to diminish some of the journals
that these have been published

00:15:39.305 --> 00:15:42.140
in, but just to say that, again,
there are so many variables

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.320
associated with a practice such
as grounding that I'll simply

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:48.770
say, yes, please do get
out of doors into nature.

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:52.880
I try every Sunday to do my zone
2 cardio by rucking or jogging

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:55.010
or hiking, often
with other people

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.710
if I'm trying to be social
with family or others.

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:59.960
But the point is
getting out of doors

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:02.247
has myriad positive
effects on mental health

00:16:02.247 --> 00:16:03.080
and physical health.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:04.550
And of course, when you're
moving out of doors,

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:07.080
you're also getting that
zone 2 cardio or other forms

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.475
of physical benefit by
elevating your heart rate

00:16:09.475 --> 00:16:11.850
or perhaps you could even do
your resistance training out

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:12.930
of doors on other days.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:16.560
Now, I also try to get out of
doors other days of the week.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:19.290
But oftentimes, I'm,
by way of weather

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:21.660
or by way of other commitments,
forced to be indoors,

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.150
on planes, here at
the podcast studio

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:25.650
where certainly I'm indoors.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.290
But I try and get out of
doors at least a few minutes

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:31.390
each day for a morning stroll,
looking at sunlight, et cetera.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.920
So the long and short
of this is, yes, there's

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:36.240
some evidence for grounding.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:37.590
Is it super strong evidence?

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:38.670
No, it's not.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.760
We don't really know what it
is about placing one's feet

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:44.488
onto the Earth that is producing
the positive effects that

00:16:44.488 --> 00:16:45.780
were observed in those studies.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:47.910
And those studies made
some reasonable attempt

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:50.160
to isolate the variables and
figure out whether or not

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.597
it was ion exchange with
the Earth or the tactile,

00:16:52.597 --> 00:16:54.180
meaning the touch
sensation, of having

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:55.392
one's feet on the ground.

00:16:55.392 --> 00:16:57.600
Frankly, I don't think
there's enough quality science

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:00.160
to really draw any firm
conclusions about that.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:03.875
However, if you like the idea of
grounding, by all means, do it.

00:17:03.875 --> 00:17:05.250
In fact, if it
feels good to you,

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.125
I recommend getting your
morning sunlight out

00:17:07.125 --> 00:17:09.450
of doors with your bare
feet on the ground.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.500
Or if you're like me,
you put on your shoes,

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:12.750
and you take a walk most days.

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:14.750
Although, I've tried this
practice of grounding,

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:16.470
and it feels pretty
good, meaning

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.329
it feels nice to have
my feet on the Earth,

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:21.690
provided I'm on clean
soil or clean lawn.

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:23.670
Definitely don't do
this at the dog park.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.160
Hookworm is a real
thing, by the way, folks.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.230
So pay attention to
the sorts of surfaces

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:29.700
that you're putting
your feet onto.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.100
But the question about
whether or not nature

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:35.680
is valuable for our mental and
physical health is an easy one.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:36.780
It's an absolute yes.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:39.720
But isolating the particular
variables about nature

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.170
that are most beneficial, well,
that's a much tougher question,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:46.180
and it's one that, frankly,
the scientific method is not

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:48.120
and, to be honest,
I don't think ever

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:50.940
will be in a position
to isolate and really

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.490
nail down specifically, because
as soon as you get specific

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:56.280
about that question,
you start to diminish

00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:58.120
the value of the study itself.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:01.350
So the long and short of
this is get out into nature

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:03.000
as often as you safely can.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.610
If you can exercise out
of doors, even better.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.240
If you want to make
it social, great.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:09.150
If you don't want to
make it social, fine.

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:09.780
It's your life.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:10.590
It's up to you.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:13.620
But there certainly is value
in getting out into nature.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:16.470
It's also just beautiful
from a visual perspective,

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.120
from an auditory perspective.

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:21.123
And I myself try and take at
least a few trips each year.

00:18:21.123 --> 00:18:23.040
None of these are
particularly expensive trips

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.410
where I try and get
out hiking, camping.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:29.520
The weekly walks in nature
are an absolute must for me.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:32.397
If I miss one because of
weather conditions or travel,

00:18:32.397 --> 00:18:34.230
I make it a point to
try and get into nature

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:36.940
more during the following
week or whenever I can.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:40.350
And frankly, I don't have
a scientific explanation

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:42.330
for why nature is
oh-so beneficial,

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:44.730
except for the sunlight piece
and perhaps this grounding

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:46.620
piece and the negative
ionization piece.

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:48.450
And frankly, I
don't worry so much

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:51.480
about the lack of
variable-isolating, quality,

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:54.000
peer-reviewed studies that
support the benefits of getting

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.110
out into nature.

00:18:55.110 --> 00:18:57.000
I simply like getting
out into nature

00:18:57.000 --> 00:18:58.680
and into different
natural environments

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.940
as much as I possibly can,
because, for whatever reason,

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:04.720
imagine those reasons have
something to do with serotonin,

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.780
dopamine, hormones,
oxytocin, probably

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.490
a bunch of different
things that are

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:11.070
rooted in how our
nervous system evolved

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:12.090
in natural environments.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:14.610
Well, it just feels really good.

